 ## Browser tests
 
-The tests that drive the portal in a real browser need Playwright and a browser (`pip install playwright`, then `playwright install chromium`) and are skipped without them. They are in `tests/test_portal_browser.py`, `tests/test_portal_lead_pages.py`, three in `tests/test_portal_wording_and_masking.py`, a few in `tests/test_mode_safety.py`, and the layout and look tests (`tests/test_portal_layout.py`, `tests/test_portal_look.py`), which use the same browser helpers. **The checks in `.github/workflows/lint.yml` do not install Playwright, so they do not run the browser tests;** the masking promise P10 rests on them, so run them on your own computer. To see why any test was skipped, run `python -m pytest -q -rs`.
+Some tests drive the portal in a real web browser. For that they need Playwright (a Python package that remote-controls a browser) and a copy of the Chromium browser. Without them these tests are skipped, not failed. They are in `tests/test_portal_browser.py`, `tests/test_portal_lead_pages.py`, `tests/test_stage_confirm_browser.py`, three in `tests/test_portal_wording_and_masking.py`, a few in `tests/test_mode_safety.py`, and the layout and look tests (`tests/test_portal_layout.py`, `tests/test_portal_look.py`), which use the same browser helpers. **The checks in `.github/workflows/lint.yml` install only `requirements-dev.txt` and there is no browser job yet, so they do not run the browser tests;** the masking promise P10 rests on them, so run them on your own computer.
+
+Playwright is not in the development lock files. It has its own optional lock file, `requirements-browser.txt` (Linux x86_64, Python 3.12) or `requirements-browser-win.txt` (Windows, Python 3.13), with hashes. It holds Playwright 1.63.0 and the three packages Playwright needs (greenlet, pyee and typing-extensions; typing-extensions has the same version as in the development lock). Nothing else is needed: the two sidebar tests read the colours of a screenshot inside the browser itself, so no image library such as Pillow is required. Install it after the development lock, in the same `.venv`:
+
+    python -m pip install --require-hashes -r requirements-dev.txt
+    python -m pip install --require-hashes -r requirements-browser.txt      # on Windows: requirements-browser-win.txt
+    playwright install chromium
+    export PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=<folder that holds the browser>        # only if the browser is not in its default place
+    export PATH=<folder that holds node>:$PATH                              # Node is a program that runs JavaScript; some tests run the portal's script with it
+    python -m pytest -q -rs
+
+Measured on Linux with Python 3.12: a new `.venv` with both lock files installed with `--require-hashes`, Chromium build 1243 and Node 22 already present in folders of their own (written as placeholders above), so `playwright install chromium` itself was not run there. That step on a computer without a browser was not measured, and Windows was not measured.
+
+* **The Chromium build must match the Playwright version.** A "build" is the numbered release of the browser that Playwright downloads. Playwright 1.63.0, the version in the lock, looks for build 1243, which is what `playwright install chromium` fetches for it. A newer Playwright wants a newer build (the Playwright 1.65.1 package lists build 1254); if only build 1243 is installed, it is not found and every browser test is skipped with a "BrowserType.launch: Executable doesn't exist" message. Keep the Playwright version in the lock and the installed build together.
+* **Where the browser is not in the default place,** set `PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH` to the folder that holds it: the tests start the browser with no path of their own.
+* **Counts from earlier full runs, each on its own commit (Linux, Python 3.12):** without Playwright, 2349 passed and 116 skipped (two earlier review runs, on commits bee6fff4 and 43110e7). With Playwright 1.63.0, the run on commit 43110e7 got 2 failed, 2460 passed and 3 skipped; the 2 failures were the two sidebar tests, which needed Pillow, so 2462 passed and 3 skipped is that run's expected figure with Pillow installed, not a measured run. The 3 skipped are: the secret scan in `tests/test_hygiene.py` (no `gitleaks` program installed), the load measurement (it runs only when `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_N` is set), and one test that runs only on Windows.
+* **A full run on this lock (Linux, Python 3.12, commit b29bb1f plus the two browser lock files, Playwright 1.63.0, Chromium build 1243, Node 22, one serial process, 2465 tests):** 2462 passed and 3 skipped in 17 minutes, no failure. The 3 skips are the same three as above. This is the measured figure for the sidebar tests too: with the browser reading the pixels no image library is installed, and they pass.
+* **Skips reported without Playwright:** 113 of the 116 are browser tests, counted by the line where the skip is reported, not by test file (22 at lines in `test_mode_safety.py`, 28 in `test_portal_browser.py`, 60 in `test_portal_lead_pages.py`, 3 in `test_portal_wording_and_masking.py`; the helper functions that skip sit in those files, so tests from other browser files are counted there too).
+* **A count of 2329 passed and 135 skipped (from the project owner's own computer):** its cause is not known. What fits the numbers, as an inference only: a run without Node on the path. Node missing makes 21 tests that run the portal's JavaScript skip (1 in `test_mode_safety.py`, 2 in `test_portal_lead_pages.py`, 18 in `test_portal_mode.py`; measured in a browserless run of those eight files here: 134 skipped with Node missing, 113 with Node present). Moving those 21 from passed to skipped on a full run of this commit gives 2328 passed and 137 skipped, against 2329 and 135: one more passed and two fewer skipped. What is unknown: that computer's operating system, whether `gitleaks` was installed there, whether Node was present, and the exact commit. Neither "one test fewer" nor "gitleaks installed" alone fits both numbers. The 2462 passed with 2 skipped reported for that computer with Playwright is not explained either (with `gitleaks` installed it would be 2463 passed here). The 2349/116 run on 43110e7 did have Node (its log has no "node is not installed" skip), even though its summary says otherwise. If you see a count near 2329/135, run `python -m pytest -q -rs` and look for "node is not installed".
+
+## Quality checks and what to do when a check fails
+
+The commands for ruff, mypy, bandit and the schema check are listed under "Quality commands" in `contract.md`, and the same commands, in order, are in `.github/workflows/lint.yml` (that file also has the secret scan and the scan for hardcoded literals). Run them from the repository folder with the `.venv` folder switched on.
+
+When a check fails: read the last lines of its output first; they name the file and line. For a test, run it alone (`python -m pytest -q tests/test_name.py::test_name`). A test that did not run is not a pass: to see why tests were skipped, run `python -m pytest -q -rs`, which prints one line per skip reason (for example "could not import 'playwright.sync_api'", "node is not installed", "no gitleaks program on PATH"). To make a missing secret scan a failure instead of a skip, set `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN=1` and `GITLEAKS_BIN` to the program's path.
 
